<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=x-ray">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">X-ray</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1895">1895</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as X-radiation, Röntgen rays, Roentgen rays</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An X-ray is a form of high-energy electromagnetic radiation, with a wavelength shorter than ultraviolet rays and longer than gamma rays, discovered by the German physicist Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen on 8 November 1895. He named it X-radiation to signify an unknown type of radiation and received the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics for the discovery. Because X-rays pass through living tissue, they were taken up in medicine within weeks: John Hall-Edwards first used them under clinical conditions in Birmingham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, on 11 January 1896. Being ionising, they can also cause burns and cancer.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/x-ray.jpg" alt="X-ray" width="320" height="501" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilhelm Röntgen; current version created by Old Moonraker. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:X-ray_by_Wilhelm_R%C3%B6ntgen_of_Albert_von_K%C3%B6lliker's_hand_-_18960123-02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
